(* --- Tables --- *)
table = pipe_table | delimited_table ;
pipe_table = standard_row, [delimiter_row], {table_row}, [caption_slot] ;
delimited_table = fenced_code_block, [caption_slot] ;
(* `delimited_table` is selected ONLY when a preceding block-attribute run
   carries the boolean `table` key and the fence info language is exactly
   `csv` or `tsv`. Without that key the same fence is a `code_block`; with a
   different language it remains a `code_block`. The attribute is a parser
   directive, consumed rather than emitted on the table. See PART 9 §5. *)
(* a table BEGINS with a standard row; a continuation row only ever follows
   an existing row (its cells append to the row above -- PART 9 §5). An optional
   GFM delimiter row may appear as the SECOND line; it promotes the first row to
   a header and assigns per-column alignment (PART 9 §5, GFM DELIMITER ROW) *)

table_row = standard_row | continuation_row ;

standard_row = '|', table_cell, {'|', table_cell}, '|', [row_attributes], newline ;

continuation_row = '+', table_cell, {'|', table_cell}, '|', newline ;

(* THE ROW TERMINATOR AND AN OPEN RUN -- NORMATIVE [CARVE-P2-019].
   Cells are cut from the line BEFORE any inline parsing, so what an inline run
   does can never decide where a row ends.

   1. AN UNCLOSED VERBATIM RUN STOPS AT THE ROW'S CLOSING `|`. The closer is the
      last `|` on the line, trailing whitespace aside. The run swallows every
      earlier `|` -- so `| a `b | c d |` is ONE cell -- and does not swallow the
      one that ends the row.

   2. A `+` CONTINUATION EXTENDS THE CELL, so a run left open by the row above
      is still open when the continuation is cut into cells: the pipes it spans
      there are its content too, and the block it reaches the end of is the
      whole cell.

   3. AN ESCAPED CLOSING PIPE IS STILL AN ESCAPE. `| a b \|` ends in a pipe, so
      the row closes there; the escape decides what the CELL holds, which is a
      literal pipe rather than an orphaned backslash -- `\|` is the only way to
      write a literal pipe in a cell, in every position of the row. This does
      not widen what a row is: `| a | b`, with content dangling after the last
      pipe, is prose exactly as before. An attribute value is one of those
      positions: a writer spells a value holding `|` quoted, as
      `{k="a\|b"}`, and the quoted value reads the escape back as the pipe
. *)

(* Row-level attributes: a `{…}` attribute block GLUED to the row's closing `|`
   (no intervening space) sets the row's `<tr>` attributes -- the row-level twin
   of a cell's attribute block (cell_attributes). The two are NOT in the same
   position: a row's block follows the row's LAST `|`, a cell's follows the
   cell's marker run (CELL ATTRIBUTES below). The whole
   payload must be valid attribute syntax (PART 9 §15); otherwise the `{` is ordinary
   content and the line is not a row attribute (see PART 9 §5, ROW
   ATTRIBUTES). *)
row_attributes = attributes ;

(* GFM delimiter row (alternative header syntax to the native `|=` header cell).
   Only recognized as the row IMMEDIATELY AFTER the first row of a table; every
   cell must be a delimiter_cell. A delimiter-shaped row in any OTHER position
   (the first line, or any row after the second) is an ordinary standard_row --
   its `-` runs are inline content (smart typography may render `---` as an em
   dash). See PART 9 §5 (GFM DELIMITER ROW) for header promotion + alignment. *)
delimiter_row = '|', delimiter_cell, {'|', delimiter_cell}, '|', newline ;
delimiter_cell = {space}, [':'], '-', {'-'}, [':'], {space} ;
(* at least one '-'; only optional leading/trailing ':' and surrounding
   padding. An EMPTY cell is NOT a delimiter_cell, so a row containing one
   (`|---||`) is not a delimiter row (it is an ordinary row). *)

table_cell = header_cell | data_cell | span_cell ;

header_cell = '=', [alignment_run], [cell_attributes], cell_padding, cell_content, {space} ;
data_cell   =      [alignment_run], [cell_attributes], cell_padding, cell_content, {space} ;
(* THE MARKER RUN COMES FIRST, IN ONE ORDER, IN BOTH PRODUCTIONS. The kind
   marker `=` and then the alignment marker are glued to the opening `|` (no
   preceding whitespace); the attribute block attaches after them. A
   cell containing only '^' or '<', with any space padding or none, is span_cell.

   A marker run ends with one space (PART 9 §5 T11). A cell with no run
   accepts unpadded content (`|a|`). *)
(* CELL ATTRIBUTES (NORMATIVE): an attribute block sets the cell's attributes.
   It binds last (PART 9 §5 T10) and is glued to the marker run or the
   opening `|` (`|=<{.x} …`, `|{.x} …`). The rest of the cell, after
   optional whitespace, is the content. A space before the brace (`| {.x}`) is
   ordinary content, NOT attributes. The whole brace payload must be valid
   attribute syntax (PART 9 §15); otherwise the '{' is literal content. A cell
   carrying attributes is never a bare span_cell -- its content is literal even
   if it is just '^' or '<', AND THE ATTRIBUTES STAY ON IT. The
   `|{.x} < |` is `<td class="x">&lt;</td>`; `| {.x} < |` has ordinary
   text `{.x} <` because the space precedes the brace.
   A computed rowspan/colspan/alignment is authoritative, so an author copy of
   those keys is dropped.

   `|{#x}=R|` is a data cell whose content is `{#x}=R`; no marker run ends
   without a space (T11). `|{#x} =R |` has id `x` and content `=R`. *)
cell_attributes = attributes ;
span_cell = rowspan_marker | colspan_marker ;

rowspan_marker = {space}, '^', {space} ;
colspan_marker = {space}, '<', {space} ;
(* TABLE-CELL PADDING (NORMATIVE, PART 7). Every padding slot in the five
   productions above is spelled `space`. All of them sit after the row's
   opening `|`, so all of them are INLINE, and A TAB IS SYNTAX ONLY IN THE
   LEADING RUN (CARVE-P7-001). A tab written in one of these slots is not padding:
   it stays where it is and becomes ordinary cell content, so
   `|<TAB>a<TAB>|` is a cell whose text begins and ends with a tab, and
   `|<TAB>---<TAB>|` is not a delimiter_cell at all - the row is an ordinary
   row and promotes no header. Same for the two span markers: a tab beside
   the `^` or `<` makes the cell ordinary content rather than a span. So a
   cell is EMPTY only when nothing but space padding stands in it: `|<TAB>|`
   holds a tab, while `| |` and `||` are empty (PART 9 §5 CARVE-P9-082). *)

alignment_run = horizontal_alignment, [vertical_alignment]
              | inherited_horizontal_alignment, vertical_alignment ;
horizontal_alignment = '<' | '>' | '~' ;  (* left, right, center *)
vertical_alignment = '^' | '~' | 'v' ;    (* top, middle, bottom *)
inherited_horizontal_alignment = '?' ;
cell_padding = space | (* empty only when the cell carries no marker run *) ;
(* An alignment run is a horizontal marker, optionally followed by a vertical
   marker. Its ending space follows PART 9 §5 T11. A
   vertical marker therefore never stands
   alone: `|^ x` and `|v x` keep `^`/`v` as visible content.
   Reverse-order pairs are invalid. Duplicate axes are invalid, except `~~`,
   whose first marker is horizontal center and second is vertical middle.
   `?` contributes no cell-level horizontal value: `?^`, `?~` and `?v` set
   only vertical alignment and inherit horizontal alignment from the column.
   It is valid only in that leading two-marker form. A lone `~` is horizontal
   center; no vertical value has a one-marker spelling.
   With no alignment run, an unpadded cell follows the `data_cell` production. *)

cell_content = [inline_content] ;
(* CELL CONTENT MAY BE EMPTY. `inline_content` is one-or-more, so the option
   is what lets `| |`, `||` and `|||` be rows of empty cells. A row is valid
   by its delimiters, never by what its cells hold (PART 9 §5
   CARVE-P9-082); an empty marked cell still needs `cell_padding`, so `|= |`
   is an empty header cell and `|=|` a data cell holding `=`.
   SEMANTIC CONSTRAINT (PART 9 §5): cell content may not contain an
   unescaped `|` outside a code span -- the pipe is the cell separator;
   the exception is not expressible context-free. *)

(* --- Captions --- *)
caption = '^', space+, inline_content, newline, {caption_continuation_line} ;
caption_continuation_line = inline_content, newline ;  (* a PLAIN line only *)
(* THE CAPTION MARKER SEPARATOR IS A RUN -- NORMATIVE [CARVE-P2-020]. `space+`: one or more
   ASCII spaces after the caret, ALL of them separator, and the first character
   that is not one BEGINS the caption. `^<SP><SP><SP>cap` is the caption `cap`,
   not `<SP><SP>cap`.

   TWO CLAUSES DECIDE IT. CARVE-P7-002 makes a
   marker separator what stands between the marker and the content it
   introduces, and the four slots it narrows to exactly one space are PADDING
   slots -- a closed enumeration the caret is not in. And PART 11 §1 names
   MARKER ALIGNMENT among the spellings `fmt` may normalize, which holds only
   if the run is not content.

   A TAB IS NOT SEPARATOR. `space = ' '`, so `^<SP><TAB>cap` keeps the tab as
   the caption's first character. MARKER REQUIRES CONTENT (CARVE-P2-009) applies
   after the run as well: a caret, the separator and nothing but whitespace
   opens no caption, and the line is ordinary paragraph text.

   THE CONTINUATION LINE IS NOT TOUCHED BY THIS. A continuation line carries no
   marker, so its leading run is INDENTATION and it is kept as written. *)
(* MULTI-LINE CAPTIONS -- NORMATIVE [CARVE-P2-021]. A caption is multi-line inline CONTENT, so
   its text spills onto following lines exactly like a PARAGRAPH (PART 9 §10), NOT like
   a heading. It ends the same way an open paragraph does:
     1. a blank line ends the caption;
     2. a line that INTERRUPTS a paragraph -- a heading, blockquote, table,
        fenced code, `:::` div, thematic break, or `%%%` comment -- ends the
        caption and starts that block;
     3. a LIST MARKER does NOT end the caption (PART 9 §10 I2): `^ cap` + `- x`
        yields the caption `cap\n- x`;
     4. a further `^ ` line does NOT continue the caption (there is no repeated
        marker); it ends the caption and, having no captionable block to attach
        to, is ordinary paragraph text.
   A further `^ ` line begins its own paragraph after the caption slot even
   when the preceding reference image does not resolve. Resolution returns
   unbound caption source as text within the block structure already parsed;
   it does not move that paragraph boundary. A later reference definition
   therefore changes the image and caption rendering, not this boundary.
   Continuation lines join with a newline, so `^ cap` + `more` yields the
   caption text `cap\nmore`. The caption id (`#` placeholder) and cross-reference
   targeting use the full folded text. *)
caption_slot = [blank_line], caption ;
(* FORMAL adjacency (PART 9 §4): a caption attaches to the immediately
   preceding captionable block, with AT MOST ONE blank line between them --
   the optional blank_line in caption_slot is the whole allowance.

   FIVE OF THE SEVEN HOSTS REFERENCE THIS SLOT: `code_block`, `blockquote`,
   `table`, `figure_group` and `quote_block`; the last two attach it to their
   CLOSING fence (CARVE-P9-001, PART 9 §4). Image paragraphs and standalone display-math blocks
   follow the same allowance under PART 9 §4 without a slot-carrying production.
   Any other `^ ` line is ordinary inline/paragraph content and, when a
   paragraph is OPEN above it, folds into it (PART 9 §10 I7).

   The optional `blank_line` matches a TRULY blank line only. A
   structural-only line -- a bare `>`, a bare list marker, a bare
   continuation marker, a bare fence -- is not blank, and where it changes
   the open container it ends the caption scope instead of being spent as
   the allowance (CARVE-P9-080). *)

(* CAPTION NUMBER PLACEHOLDER -- NORMATIVE [CARVE-P2-022].
   The FIRST bare `#` in a caption's TOP-LEVEL inline_content is a number
   placeholder. "Bare" = a `#` that does NOT begin a tag (a `#`
   followed by whitespace, `:`, `.`, end-of-caption, or any
   non-tag-name character). `#word` stays a tag (PART 9 §19). `\#` is
   a literal `#`, never a placeholder. Only the first bare `#` is a
   placeholder; later bare `#` render literally. A `#` INSIDE inline
   markup (emphasis, link, span) is NOT a placeholder -- put it in the
   caption's top-level text (`^ *Figure* #:`, not `^ *Figure #*:`).
   The LABEL is the inline_content before the placeholder, trailing
   whitespace trimmed; the counter BUCKET KEY is that label's plain
   text. Numbering is per-bucket, 1-based, in document order, assigned
   in the resolution pass. A caption with no bare `#` is unchanged. *)

(* --- Admonitions --- *)
(* A fence is a run of 3+ colons, for every colon container alike. The run
   length is a DEPTH count, not a quoting device. *)
colon_fence = ":::", {":"} ;
(* The colon fence has ONE separator slot -- the whitespace immediately after
   the fence run -- and all four openers share it. It is a MARKER SEPARATOR
   (CARVE-P7-002): the token after it selects which of the four blocks the line
   opens, so it is spelled `space` and a tab does not satisfy it. `div_open`
   makes the slot OPTIONAL because a bare `[label]` may sit flush against the
   fence (`:::[First]`); optional is a different property from a different
   role, and the slot answers the same way in all four openers whichever type
   token follows it.  *)
colon_fence_close = colon_fence:close
                    where len(close) = len(open) ;
(* FORMAL (stated as a guard): `open` is the colon_fence captured by
   the INNERMOST open block's opener (admonition_open / div_open /
   line_block_open / local_hard_break_block_open). A block is closed only by
   a bare fence of EXACTLY its opener's length.

   Consequences, all of which follow from the one guard:
     - equal-length fences NEST (`::: note` may hold `::: tip`);
     - a bare fence whose length does not match the innermost open block is
       not a closer at all -- it is an ordinary opener, and opens a block;
     - which direction the lengths run is therefore free. Longer-outer
       documents and longer-inner ones both parse. `carve fmt` emits the
       inward-widening form: the outermost container is `:::` and each level
       inward adds one colon. The writer sizes each fence from the depth it
       is already carrying, with no lookahead, and the widths along any one
       chain are strictly increasing.

   Code fences keep >= (PART 9 §2) because their length axis really is
   quoting: opaque content that never nests, which must be able to hold a
   shorter fence. *)

admonition = admonition_open, newline,
             {block - admonition_close},
             [admonition_close, newline] ;
(* the body may be EMPTY, like the generic div's (PART 9 §12).

   UNCLOSED. The closer is OPTIONAL: an opener always opens, bare or labeled, and
   a block still open when its HOST ends closes there, innermost first. The
   host is the ENCLOSING CONTAINER where there is one and the DOCUMENT
   otherwise, so "closes at end of input" is the top-level case rather than
   the whole rule: a `::: note` opened inside a block quote and never closed
   ends WITH THE QUOTE. A flush-left line below that cannot continue an open
   paragraph lazily, for example after a blank line, is a sibling paragraph
   outside both. An unclosed opener SHOULD be
   diagnosed by lint and by the language server; it is not an error for the
   parser. Note this is NOT the `%%%` comment-block rule (CARVE-P9-060), where an
   opener with no exact closer ahead opens nothing: a comment block is
   invisible either way, so failing closed costs nothing there.

   A MARKER-LINE OPENER OPENS TOO. A `:::` opener written as the sole content
   of a list item's marker line opens its container whatever the next line
   is. In `- ::: d` / `tail` the item holds an empty `d` container, which
   leaves no paragraph open, and `tail` is a document paragraph
   (CARVE-P0-009, CARVE-P0-016).

   OPAQUE SPANS. `{block - admonition_close}` is a BLOCK
   sequence, so a fenced code block, a raw block and a comment block inside
   the body are single blocks and their lines are never examined for a
   closer. A colon fence written inside one is content: it neither closes the
   container nor opens a child. An implementation that collects the body by
   scanning raw lines has to model this explicitly, and must consume the
   span's opener BEFORE testing for its closer -- an opener with no info
   string (a bare code fence, a `%%%`) is closer-shaped itself, so testing it
   first ends the span on its own line and hands the span's contents back to
   the block parser.

   TOOLING FOR NEAR MATCHES. Exact length remains a parse rule,
   not a recoverable syntax error. A processor with diagnostics SHOULD report
   `colon-fence-length-mismatch` when a bare colon run does not close the
   innermost open container, would close it after resizing, and plausibly
   expresses closing intent. The finding names the authored and expected
   widths, the opener position, and the line's actual parse outcome. It MUST
   NOT fire for typed openers, escaped colons, opaque code/raw/comment
   payloads, or a different-width child fence that has its own exact closer.

   A tool MAY offer separate fixes to resize the run or preserve it as literal
   content. Neither is a canonical-formatting decision: `carve fmt` MUST NOT
   choose an intention. Editors SHOULD pair and highlight exact opener/closer
   ranges from parser structure; proximity alone never licenses resizing a
   nested fence.

   A ``` or ~~~ opener with no code closer ahead opens a code block that ends
   at the innermost container's exact-length closer. That closer stays
   structural. Every other colon line before it is code content, and lines
   after it parse normally. In

       ::: note
       ```
       x
       :::
       after

   the `:::` closes the admonition and `after` is a top-level paragraph.

   This does NOT change a fence with nothing to interrupt: `` ``` `` alone,
   or as the whole content of a blockquote, still opens a code block that
   runs to the end. PART 9 §10 I4 and this clause both gate a fence that would take
   structure away from something else, not a fence that is the content.
   Nor does it reach a ``` or ~~~ line that continues an open paragraph:
   under PART 9 §10 I4 that line is paragraph text, not an opener, so the
   colon lines after it are read under PART 2 as usual. *)

(* STRICT (djot): the opener line carries NO inline `{...}` attributes -- it
   is colon_fence, type, an optional quoted "header", an optional [label],
   and NOTHING else. Metadata outside that shape remains invalid. Without a
   type word the line is paragraph text: `::: {.x}` and `:::{k=v}`. Recovery
   recognizes a colon fence followed by `space+` and an ASCII type word.
   The type word must end at the line end, Unicode White_Space or U+FEFF,
   a straight double quote, `{`, `[`, or a curly double quote. Recovery is
   confined to one source line. It reports fence-title-syntax, drops all opener metadata, and parses the children as
   blocks through the matching-width closer: `::: note {.x}` opens
   the `note` container with no metadata. This recovery does
   not admit a bare title as valid syntax. An unrecognized fence/type prefix
   remains paragraph text.
   Class/id/data-* attach via a PRECEDING block-attribute line, which floats
   onto the admonition (PART 9 §15).
   The "header" and [label] are the SAME two metadata tokens a code fence
   takes (PART 9 §2), in the same fixed order:
     - HEADER ("...") -- the visible admonition title (`<p
       class="admonition-title">`, or `<summary>` under the details
       extension). Unchanged role.
     - LABEL ([...]) -- a short grouping identifier; core ignores it standalone,
       a group extension (e.g. tabs) uses it as the tab label. This is the
       canonical replacement for the tabs `{label="..."}` / inner-heading
       convention (those stay supported, deprecated). The `selected`
       default-tab marker is NOT a label -- it remains a boolean attribute on
       the preceding `{...}` line. *)
(* The same line carries both roles, and both take `space`. The first
   separator is what makes this an admonition rather than a div, a line block
   or a local hard-break block, so it is a MARKER SEPARATOR; once
   `admonition_type` has been read the block is decided and the "title" and
   [label] slots are ordinary inter-token padding (CARVE-P7-002). The roles
   differ, the terminal does not, because A TAB IS SYNTAX ONLY IN THE LEADING
   RUN (CARVE-P7-001). `::: note<TAB>"T"` is not a
   valid admonition opener. It recovers as the named container with no
   metadata and a diagnostic. A tab in the marker-separator slot still leaves
   the prefix unrecognized and the line as prose. *)
admonition_open = colon_fence:open, space, admonition_type,
                  [space+, quoted_title], [space+, label] ;
admonition_close = colon_fence_close ;

admonition_type = "note" | "tip" | "warning" | "danger" | "info"
                | "success" | "example" | "quote"  (* Tier 1 canonical *)
                | explicit_identifier ;  (* Tier 2 custom types (incl. `details`)
                                   -- see PART 9 §12 for the two-tier
                                   rendering rule *)

quoted_title = '"', {character - '"'}, '"' ;
(* there is NO escape mechanism inside a quoted title -- a title cannot
   contain a `"`; a malformed title is diagnosed and dropped while its recognized
   container keeps its children. Unlike `quoted_value`, this
   token does not accept escapes *)

(* --- Composite figures (CARVE-P9-001) --- *)
(* The kind word `figure` is RESERVED among the `:::` types: a BARE opener --
   fence, separator, the word `figure`, and NOTHING else -- opens a composite
   figure rather than an admonition. `figure_group_open` wins over
   `admonition_open` on exactly that line; an opener that carries a quoted
   title or a `[label]` (`::: figure "T"`, `::: figure [g]`) does NOT match
   this production and stays an `admonition` of kind `figure` -- a generic
   Tier-2 container, metadata preserved losslessly -- which `carve lint`
   reports (CARVE-P9-001). `figure_group` carries no title and no label BY
   DESIGN: the group's one piece of authored metadata is its caption, and the
   caption sits after the closer, where PART 9 §4's rule already lives.

   THE CLOSER CARRIES THE CAPTION SLOT. A figure group is one of PART 9 §4's
   seven captionable hosts, and its allowance is STRUCTURAL on a fence: the
   slot hangs on the CLOSING fence, exactly the `[caption_slot]` that `code_block`, `blockquote` and
   `table` end in, with the same one-optional-blank-line allowance. Two
   container kinds take it -- this one and `quote_block`, which captions like
   the block quote it is (PART 9 §4) -- and a `^ ` line after any OTHER `:::` closer
   is ordinary paragraph content. A group with
   no closer closes with its host like any container (UNCLOSED, under
   `admonition`) and, its closer line never having been written, has no
   caption position.

   Body, closer discipline, opaque spans, fence lengths and nesting are the
   container rules, unchanged (`admonition`, above). What its direct children MEAN --
   which of them are panels, how the group numbers, how a nested bare
   `::: figure` degrades -- is CARVE-P9-001. *)
figure_group = figure_group_open, newline,
               {block - admonition_close},
               [admonition_close, newline, [caption_slot]] ;
figure_group_open = colon_fence:open, space, "figure" ;
(* the optional `caption_slot` begins on the line after the closer, at most
   one blank line below it -- caption_slot's own optional blank_line IS the
   allowance (PART 9 §4). Two blank lines detach, and the `^ ` line is an ordinary
   paragraph. *)

(* --- Generic Divs (djot generic container; PART 9 §12) --- *)
(* A BARE `:::` opener with NO type word is a generic div, NOT an
   admonition (no class added). The opener carries NO inline `{...}`
   attributes (STRICT, under `admonition_open`): `::: {…}` is a paragraph, not
   a div. A typeless div MAY still carry a [label] (e.g.
   `::: [First]` for a tab member with no semantic type); a header without a
   type is not meaningful, so `::: "x"` is a paragraph. As the FIRST token after
   the fence the [label] may sit directly against it (`:::[First]`), exactly as
   a code fence admits a bare `[label]` (a label AFTER a type word still needs a
   space -- admonition_open). Shares the colon-fence closer and the fence-length
   nesting rule (`colon_fence_close`). *)
div = div_open, newline, {block - admonition_close}, [admonition_close, newline] ;
div_open = colon_fence:open, [[space], label] ;

(* --- Line block (verse) -- a RECOGNIZED `:::` type that preserves the
   author's per-line layout. `::: |` renders as a generic
   `<div class="line-block">` (NOT an `<aside>`), but unlike an ordinary div
   its body keeps each line's LEADING WHITESPACE and turns each soft line
   break into a hard break. The type token is a bare pipe `|` on the opener --
   NOT a per-line prefix -- so it is free of the pipe/table ambiguity of the
   Pandoc/djot per-line `|` form, and uses no English keyword (jgm/djot#29).
   The behavior keys off the `|` token. The opener carries no inline
   attributes (STRICT, under `admonition_open`): `::: {.line-block}` is a
   PARAGRAPH, not a line block. Normative rendering: PART 9 §23. *)
line_block = line_block_open, newline, line_block_body, [admonition_close, newline] ;
line_block_open = colon_fence:open, space, "|" ;
(* The body is a sequence of stanzas separated by blank lines; each stanza is
   a run of consecutive non-blank lines. Inline content parses normally; the
   per-line leading whitespace is retained and the intra-stanza newlines are
   hard breaks (PART 9 §23). *)
line_block_body = stanza, {blank_line, stanza} ;
stanza = line_block_line, {line_block_line} ;
line_block_line = {whitespace}, inline_content, newline ;
(* the leading whitespace is PRESERVED in output -- PART 9 §23 *)
(* A PAYLOAD LINE IS INLINE CONTENT WHATEVER IT LOOKS LIKE AND WHATEVER HOSTS
   THE BLOCK. A line shaped like a fence run, a heading or a table row opens
   nothing, so an unclosed line block takes a following lazy line after it
   exactly as after a plain `x`, and two fence runs past the content column
   pair as one inline span in every payload, not only on a marker line. *)

(* --- Local hard-break block -- a RECOGNIZED `:::` type for authors who want
   Markdown-like visible line breaks locally without the full line-block verse
   model. `::: \` renders as `<div class="hardbreaks">` and turns soft breaks
   in DIRECT paragraph children into hard breaks. It does NOT preserve leading
   whitespace and it does NOT inherit into nested block content. *)
local_hard_break_block = local_hard_break_block_open, newline,
                         {block - admonition_close},
                         [admonition_close, newline] ;
local_hard_break_block_open = colon_fence:open, space, backslash ;

(* --- Fenced block quote (CARVE-P9-015: the container rules govern it) --- *)
(* A SECOND SPELLING of the block quote, not a second block: a colon fence
   whose type token is a bare `>` opens a quote whose body is ordinary block
   content, and the node is the one `blockquote_line` produces. The two forms
   are the same tree, so every rule that speaks of a block quote reaches both.
   Third member of the sigil-fence family beside `line_block_open` and
   `local_hard_break_block_open`, and it takes the same REQUIRED space before
   its token; `:::>` is not an opener.
   `::: quote` remains an admonition kind; `>` selects the block quote.
   WHICH SPELLING AN AUTHOR USED is carried on the node as `fenced` (PART 12),
   because `carve fmt` must be able to write back what it read: normalizing
   one spelling to the other would rewrite documents no one asked to change.
   The flag is ABSENT on a `>`-prefixed quote. *)
quote_block = quote_block_open, newline,
              {block - admonition_close},
              [admonition_close, newline, [caption_slot]] ;
quote_block_open = colon_fence:open, space, ">" ;

(* --- Comments --- *)
(* A line comment may be indented: optional leading whitespace before `%%` does
   not matter, so an indented line whose first non-whitespace content is `%%` is
   a comment line just like one in the first column. Like every block, it
   interrupts an open paragraph (the lines before and after become separate
   paragraphs) and renders nothing. *)
comment_line = [whitespace], "%%", [space], {character}, newline ;

(* A `%%%` fence line is a DELIMITER plus an INSIGNIFICANT TAIL: only the
   leading run of `%` is structural, so `%%% TODO` opens and `%%% end` closes.
   `%%%` carries NO info string -- a raw passthrough block is a CODE fence with
   an `=FORMAT` info string -- so `%%% html` is a comment, not a raw block.
   A block opens only when a matching closer exists AHEAD; otherwise the line
   degrades to a `comment_line`. Both rules are NORMATIVE in PART 9 §28. *)
comment_block = comment_block_open, newline,
                {character | newline},
                comment_block_close, newline ;  (* PART 9 §28: else comment_line *)

(* Both delimiters may be INDENTED, exactly as `comment_line` may: leading
   whitespace is not part of the delimiter, only the `%` run is, so the length
   match below compares runs and an indented closer closes an indented opener.
   PART 9 §24 C3 states the rule for both spellings. *)
comment_block_open = [whitespace], ("%%%", {'%'}):open, {character - newline} ;  (* 3+ `%`, tail ignored *)
comment_block_close = [whitespace], ("%%%", {'%'}):close, {character - newline}
                      where len(close) = len(open) ;  (* FORMAL (PART 9 §2): exact length match *)

(* trailing (inline) line comment: from a %% marker to end of line, content
   not rendered. The marker requires a space or start-of-run before it and
   is not formed inside code spans / inline-raw, nor when the first % is
   escaped -- a semantic constraint stated in PART 9, not context-free. *)
inline_comment = "%%", {character - newline} ;

(* delimited (braced) inline comment: `{%` to the first `%}`, content not
   rendered. Unlike `inline_comment` it has an explicit CLOSER, so it ends
   mid-run and may span the soft line breaks inside one paragraph. The same
   semantic constraints as the `%%` form -- never inside a code span or raw
   inline, literal when the brace is escaped -- are stated in CARVE-P9-042, and
   so is the rule that an UNTERMINATED opener stays literal text. *)
braced_comment = "{%", !(grapheme_extender), {character - "%}"}, "%}" ;
(* `{%` followed by a grapheme_extender is text, like every braced opener
   (CARVE-P3-025). *)

(* --- Raw Blocks --- *)
raw_block = code_fence_open, [space], "=", format_name, newline,
            raw_content,
            code_fence_close, newline ;
(* The opener is a code fence (backticks or tildes) whose info string is `=`
   immediately followed by a format name (```=html). The leading `=` is the
   block parallel of the inline raw `{=format}` attribute; it never starts a
   language token (a code fence's language charset excludes `=`), so this is
   unambiguous against an ordinary code block. The `=` and format name must be
   adjacent -- ```= html (space after `=`) is NOT a raw block. Leading
   whitespace before the `=` is permitted (```=html and ``` =html both open
   raw). The closer follows the PART 9 §2 rule: same fence character, length
   >= opener. Content is verbatim; it is emitted UNESCAPED when the format
   matches the output format (html) and DROPPED otherwise -- the block parallel
   of raw inline (PART 9 §20).

   THE INTERIOR IS VERBATIM, THE OPENING IS PLACED -- NORMATIVE [CARVE-P2-023]. Inside a
   container, a renderer that indents its output indents the raw block's
   OPENING position, exactly as it would any other block, and MUST NOT touch
   the raw content's own line structure. Every line after the first reaches the
   target with the columns the author gave it.

   THE PAYLOAD IS EVERY LINE BETWEEN THE DELIMITERS, blank lines included. A
   blank line is a line, and the last one is payload exactly as an interior one
   is. Neither the container the block sits in nor whether the closer was
   written is a parameter: an unterminated raw block runs to the end of
   whatever encloses it, and its payload still ends on the line before that
   end. At the END of the document the payload's trailing newlines land where
   the trailing-whitespace trim removes them.

   AN ALL-BLANK PAYLOAD IS NOT AN ABSENT BLOCK. If the sole line between the
   delimiters is blank, that line is still the payload and contributes its
   newline when the raw format matches the output format. Zero payload lines
   contribute nothing; one blank payload line contributes one newline. An
   implementation MUST NOT encode those two source shapes identically.


   A target that does not render a raw block reports `raw-format-dropped`
   (CARVE-P2-024). *)

format_name = "html" | "latex" | identifier ;
raw_content = (* any content until closing fence *) ;
